  "id": "319432673",
  "subject": [
    "ANE",
    "Assyrian"
  ],
  "title": "Kammanu/Meliddu (kingdom)",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/region"
  ],
  "details": "<p>According to A. Bagg (RGTC 7/1, 257), Kammanu (that is, Melid) was made into an Assyrian province in 712 BC by Sargon II (r. 721-705 BC). Til-garimmu, one of Melid's walled cities, became the seat of an Assyrian governor. K. Radner (RLAss 11, 42-68, esp. pp.\u00a048-49 no. 13), however, proposes that the area once occupied by Melid/Kammanu was annexed into the kingdom of Kummuhu, which in 708 BC became the Province of the Commander-in-Chief on the Left after Kummuhu was defeated by Sargon.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "A kingdom in eastern Anatolia, whose capital is classical Melitene (modern Arslantepe). Meliddu, which is sometimes referred to as Kammanu, was conquered by the eighth-century-BC Assyrian king Sargon II and its territory was given to Kummuhu.",
